RNA POLYMERASE II TRANSCRIBES Dictyostelium UNTRANSLATABLE 
GENE, dutA, SPECIFICALLY IN THE DEVELOPMENTAL PHASE

Hiroshi KUMIMOTO, Hiderou YOSHIDA and Koji OKAMOTO

Department of Botany, Faculty of Science, Kyoto University, 
Kyoto 606-01, Japan

BBRC, in press

SUMMARY

   In Dictyostelium discoideum, a novel type of RNA (dutA RNA), which
is untranslatable, cytosolic and 1.3 kb in size, appears specifically
after the aggregation stage [Yoshida, H. et al. (1994) Nucleic Acids
Res. 22, 41-46].  We here show that the dutA gene is transcribed by
RNA polymerase II, based on its a-amanitin sensitive nature of in
vitro transcriptional activity. We also show that the stage-specific
accumulation of dutA RNA is primarily due to the stage-specific
enhancement of the transcriptional activity of the gene.

